                    coach_r5_plan:          `<strong>You're blaming the plan.</strong> The strategy was sound — what failed was execution, specifically the resistance you under-treated. Don't redesign the plan; rebuild the changeability. What will you do differently with the barons now?`,
                    coach_r5_people:        `<strong>Blaming the people repeats the error.</strong> Replacing managers tells every survivor they are disposable and proves the barons right that this change is a threat. The problem is not capability; it is untreated resistance.`,
                    coach_r5_recover:       `<strong>That's the recovery.</strong> Going back to the root — a credible, status-preserving role for the barons in the new model — is what finally lowers the resistance that stalled claims. This is the move the last three programmes never made.`,
                    coach_r5_comms:         `<strong>More comms is more symptom.</strong> Another town-hall push lifts engagement numbers for a fortnight and changes nothing underneath. The barons are still telling teams to wait. Treat the root, not the readout.`,
                    coach_r5_enforce:       `<strong>Enforcement hardens the reversion.</strong> Mandates and deadlines against covert resistance produce compliance theatre — exactly the polite agreement-without-movement that buried the last three. You can't enforce changeability.`,
                    coach_r5_blameplan:     `<strong>The board hears you abandoning a sound strategy.</strong> Asking for a redesign and more time reads as the programme failing — confidence drops and the €60M is now at real risk. Own the execution gap instead.`,
                    coach_r5_optimism:      `<strong>“Essentially fine” does not survive contact with the data.</strong> The board can see claims stalling. Reassurance without a credible diagnosis of what stalled erodes the confidence you need to protect the funding.`,

                    sum_default:            `Move made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read beneath the 78%; named the psychodynamic constraint.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Trusted the survey or mis-named the constraint.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Named the status threat; mapped the quiet barons.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Chased the noise or mistook the threat.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Co-opted the Division Head; treated the root.`,
                    sum_r3_crush:           `Tried to crush informal power; the barons dug in.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Treated symptoms; the root persists.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Concentrated on one workstream first; banked an early win.`,
                    sum_r4_overload:        `Big-bang launch overloaded a low-capacity org.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Mis-sequenced; momentum or capacity lost.`,

                    insight_landed:         `Change landed: you read the organisation's changeability, surfaced the resistance the 78% hid, worked with informal power instead of crushing it, and held the board's funding through the checkpoint. Aubène 2028 is the programme that finally stuck — not the fourth in the graveyard.`,
                    insight_compliance:     `Surface compliance: you kept the funding but the hidden resistance is still high. The engagement numbers look better than the change is — exactly the polite agreement-without-movement that buried the last three. Without treating the root, this snaps back the moment you look away.`,
                    insight_paused:         `Funding paused: board confidence fell below 50 at the month-9 checkpoint. Whether from a big-bang overload, spent political capital, or blaming the plan, the board lost faith — and a paused transformation is a dying one.`,
                    insight_partial:        `A partial result: some changeability built, but the resistance isn't broken and the position is fragile. The patterns of the last three programmes are still visible — quiet delay, reversion, the pull back to the old ways.`,
                    insight_rootuntreated:  `You never treated the barons' root threat. Comms, wins and incentives move the surface; the psychodynamic root — status, identity, control — is what quietly stalls execution. This is the error that buried three predecessors.`,
                    insight_division:       `You didn't bring the Division Head with you. She controls the branch network you execute through; without her as a sponsor, every move runs uphill against the informal power you needed on your side.`,
                    insight_overload:       `You overloaded a low-capacity organisation. Launching everything at once across three fronts is how change snaps back — the org that killed three programmes had no slack to absorb a fourth all at once.`,
                    insight_capitalspent:   `You spent your political capital. Confrontation and crushing moves feel decisive but burn the room you need for the hard parts — and you arrived at the checkpoint with nothing left to spend.`,
                    insight_changeability:  `Genuine changeability: you co-opted informal power, treated the psychodynamic root, sequenced for absorptive capacity and recovered at the checkpoint by rebuilding capacity rather than redesigning a sound plan. This is Jarrett's lens applied — execution, not strategy, won it.`,

                    hint_funding:           `Board confidence is below 50; at the month-9 checkpoint that pauses the €60M. The board reads your diagnosis as closely as your results.`,
